Performing a spatial convergence test in Mathematica for your equation \( u_t = 0.03 u_{xx} + u^3 \) involves running the simulation with different spatial resolutions and comparing the results to see if they converge to a stable solution. Here's a step-by-step guide on how to do this:

### Step 1: Define the Equation and Initial/Boundary Conditions

First, define the partial differential equation (PDE) and the initial and boundary conditions.

```mathematica
eqn = D[u[t, x], t] == 0.03 D[u[t, x], {x, 2}] + u[t, x]^3;
initialCondition = u[0, x] == Sin[Pi x];
boundaryConditions = {u[t, 0] == 0, u[t, 1] == 0};
```

### Step 2: Set Up the Simulation with Different Spatial Resolutions

Define a function to solve the PDE with a specified number of grid points (`nPoints`). This will allow you to vary the spatial resolution.

```mathematica
solvePDE[nPoints_] := Module[
  {sol},
  sol = NDSolve[
    {eqn, initialCondition, boundaryConditions},
    u,
    {t, 0, 1},
    {x, 0, 1},
    Method -> {"MethodOfLines",
      "SpatialDiscretization" -> {"TensorProductGrid",
        "MaxPoints" -> nPoints,
        "MinPoints" -> nPoints,
        "DifferenceOrder" -> 4}
    }
  ];
  u /. First[sol]
]
```

### Step 3: Run the Simulation with Different Resolutions

Run the simulation with different numbers of grid points and store the solutions.

```mathematica
resolutions = {50, 100, 200, 400};
solutions = Table[solvePDE[n], {n, resolutions}];
```

### Step 4: Compare the Solutions

To compare the solutions, you can plot them at a specific time and observe how they converge as the number of grid points increases.

```mathematica
time = 0.3;
plots = Table[
  Plot[solutions[[i]][time, x], {x, 0, 1},
    PlotRange -> All,
    PlotStyle -> ColorData[97][i],
    PlotLabel -> "Solution at t = " <> ToString[time] <> ", nPoints = " <> ToString[resolutions[[i]]]
  ],
  {i, Length[resolutions]}
];
Show[plots, PlotLabel -> "Comparison of Solutions at t = " <> ToString[time]]
```

### Step 5: Quantify the Convergence

To quantify the convergence, you can compute the L2 norm of the difference between solutions at different resolutions. This will help you determine if the solutions are converging as the grid spacing decreases.

```mathematica
l2Norm[sol1_, sol2_, time_] := Sqrt[NIntegrate[(sol1[time, x] - sol2[time, x])^2, {x, 0, 1}]]

convergenceRates = Table[
  l2Norm[solutions[[i]], solutions[[i + 1]], time],
  {i, Length[resolutions] - 1}
];

convergenceRates
```

### Step 6: Analyze the Results

Examine the `convergenceRates` to see if the L2 norm of the difference between solutions decreases as the number of grid points increases. This indicates that your solution is converging.

### Full Example Code

